{#
	CV component
	=======================
	Macros that render the content in `_data/cv.js` for the `/cv` page. Imported in `_content/pages/cv.md` like so:
	{% from "components/units/cv/cv.njk" import cvRoles, cvEarlierRoles, cvSkills, cvProjects %}

	WHY MACROS (rather than includes)? They take the data as an argument, so nothing here depends on a global variable
	name, and each piece can be dropped anywhere in the markdown.

	WHY NO INDENTATION / BLANK LINES IN THE MACROS' OUTPUT? The page is markdown, which is processed *after* Nunjucks.
	An indented line following a blank line gets treated as a code block, and a blank line ends an HTML block early.
	The `-` whitespace control (eg. `{%- ... %}`) keeps the output as one tight block of HTML. If you tidy up
	the layout of this file, check `/cv` still renders properly (ie. no `<pre><code>` blocks in the output).

	Styles for these classes are in `_cv.scss`. A few (eg. `cv__title`, `cv__tech`) have no styles of their own,
	they're just hooks to make the markup readable and easier to target later.
#}

{#
	An organisation's name, linked when it has a `url` (see `_data/cv.js`), otherwise plain text.
	Shared by the macros below so the link markup lives in one place. Kept on a single line, as the macros
	need tight output (see above).
#}
{% macro cvOrganisation(name, url) -%}
{% if url %}<a href="{{ url }}" rel="external">{{ name }}</a>{% else %}{{ name }}{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}

{#
	Current and recent roles, each with highlights and the tech used.

	WHY AN ORDERED LIST? The roles are a sequence (newest first), so an `ol` / `li` describes them accurately. A screen reader
	announces "list, 4 items", and unstyled it reads as a numbered timeline. Each role isn't a self-contained piece of content
	(ie. an `article`) as it only makes sense as part of the whole CV, and a `div` would say nothing at all.
	Using `role="list"` with `.no-list`, as per the rest of the site, so assistive technologies still announce it as a list.
#}
{% macro cvRoles(roles) -%}
<ol class="cv__roles | no-list | flow" role="list">
{%- for role in roles %}
<li class="cv__role | flow">
<h3 class="cv__title">{{ role.title }}, <span class="cv__organisation">{{ cvOrganisation(role.organisation, role.url) }}</span></h3>
{#- `datetime` values are ISO year-months (eg. 2024-04). A role without an `end` is current, so says "Present" #}
<p class="cv__meta"><small><time datetime="{{ role.start }}">{{ role.startLabel }}</time> &ndash; {% if role.end %}<time datetime="{{ role.end }}">{{ role.endLabel }}</time>{% else %}Present{% endif %} <i aria-hidden="true">&#8275;</i> {{ role.type }}</small></p>
{#- Optional. Used when dates alone would be misleading (eg. contracts that weren't back-to-back) #}
{%- if role.note %}
<p class="cv__note"><small><em>{{ role.note }}</em></small></p>
{%- endif %}
<ul>
{%- for highlight in role.highlights %}
<li>{{ highlight }}</li>
{%- endfor %}
</ul>
<p class="cv__tech"><small><strong>Tech:</strong> {{ role.tech | join(', ') }}.</small></p>
</li>
{%- endfor %}
</ol>
{%- endmacro %}

{# Earlier roles. Kept brief (no highlights) as they're less relevant now, but still part of the story #}
{% macro cvEarlierRoles(earlierRoles) -%}
{#- Using `role="list"` with `.no-list`, as per the rest of the site, so assistive technologies still announce it as a list #}
<ul class="cv__earlier | no-list" role="list">
{%- for role in earlierRoles %}
<li><strong>
{%- if role.organisations %}{% for org in role.organisations %}{{ cvOrganisation(org.name, org.url) }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}
{%- else %}{{ cvOrganisation(role.organisation, role.url) }}{% endif -%}
</strong>: {{ role.title }} <small><em>({{ role.type }}, {{ role.period }})</em></small></li>
{%- endfor %}
</ul>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
	Personal projects. Each links out to its code, so the link text is the project's name (descriptive on its own).
	A plain unordered list, as the projects aren't in any particular sequence.
#}
{% macro cvProjects(projects) -%}
{#- Using `role="list"` with `.no-list`, as per the rest of the site, so assistive technologies still announce it as a list #}
<ul class="cv__projects | no-list | flow" role="list">
{%- for project in projects %}
<li><a href="{{ project.url }}">{{ project.name }}</a>: {{ project.description }}</li>
{%- endfor %}
</ul>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
	Skills grouped by category. A description list suits the label / values pairing.
	The `dt` / `dd` pairs are deliberately not wrapped in `div`s. Those would only exist for styling, and the
	list's grid layout (in `_cv.scss`) can place the pairs without them.
#}
{% macro cvSkills(skills) -%}
<dl class="cv__skills">
{%- for group in skills %}
<dt>{{ group.label }}</dt>
<dd>{{ group.items | join(', ') }}</dd>
{%- endfor %}
</dl>
{%- endmacro %}
